      "subject": "כי אם הסירך העורים והפסחים",
      "rationale": "The Jebusites' taunt: either the blind and lame 'will turn you away' (they suffice to repel David), or 'except he remove the blind and lame' — the idiom כי אם admits both; the context favors the taunt reading."
    },
    {
      "kind": "idiom",
      "subject": "ירושלם",
      "rationale": "The bare proper name after a verb of motion is directional ('to Jerusalem'); Hebrew marks it by position, not by a separate ending here."
    },
    {
      "kind": "alternative",
      "subject": "העורים והפסחים",
      "rationale": "The blind and the lame may be literal (the disabled manning the walls) or a mocking nickname for David's men — the taunt is deliberately ambiguous."
    }
  ],
  "translation": "And the king and his men went, Jerusalem-ward, to the Jebusite, the one dwelling in the land, and he said to David, saying, “You shall not come here, for the blind and the lame will turn you aside,” saying, “David shall not come here.”",
